<!-- 2 · The need: AI x climate cuts both ways -->
<section class="slide">
  <div class="slide-inner">
    <span class="kicker">The need · 1 of 3</span>
    <h1>AI and climate <em>cut both ways.</em></h1>
    <div class="cards cols-3">
      <div class="card stat">
        <span class="num">&gt;1,000 TWh</span>
        <span class="what">Data-centre electricity demand by 2026 — roughly doubling since 2022, comparable to all of Japan.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="card stat">
        <span class="num">33–80 Mt</span>
        <span class="what">CO₂ attributable to AI systems in 2025 alone — plus significant freshwater withdrawals for cooling.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="card stat">
        <span class="num">$1 → $10</span>
        <span class="what">Every dollar invested in AI-supported early warning saves up to ten in avoided climate losses.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="punch">Whether AI is net-positive for climate is not a property of the technology — it is a property of <strong>who governs it, where it sits, what powers it, and whose data it runs on.</strong></p>
    <p class="foot-src">Sources: IEA via Carbon Brief · Cell Reports Physical Science · UNFCCC AI4ClimateAction · Brookings</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 3 · The need: the WhatsApp problem -->
<section class="slide">
  <div class="slide-inner">
    <span class="kicker">The need · 2 of 3</span>
    <h1>The <em>WhatsApp problem.</em></h1>
    <p class="sub">Youth coordination at UNFCCC conferences runs on public and semi-public WhatsApp groups — delegation logistics, drafting, action planning. The evidence says that is a structural liability:</p>
    <div class="cards cols-3">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="tag">Links leak</span>
        <h3>400,000+ group invite links</h3>
        <p>indexed by a simple Google search in one incident — exposing groups, chats, and members' phone numbers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="tag">Joining exposes you</span>
        <h3>Every member sees every number</h3>
        <p>Encryption protects content, not metadata: who is in which group, talking to whom, when.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="tag">Conferences are watched</span>
        <h3>COP27 precedent</h3>
        <p>Civil society documented surveillance in-venue; several governments told delegates not to install the official app.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="punch">An open invite link + visible phone numbers + membership metadata = <strong>a public roster of changemakers, their networks, and their plans.</strong></p>
    <p class="foot-src">Sources: XDA Developers · Tech Advisor · heyData · Society of Environmental Journalists · Al Jazeera</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 4 · The need: infrastructure youth can own -->
<section class="slide">
  <div class="slide-inner">
    <span class="kicker">The need · 3 of 3</span>
    <h1>Who <em>owns</em> the infrastructure of coordination?</h1>
    <p class="sub">Locally led climate action has endorsed principles — devolved decisions, patient funding, local capability — but practice lags. The communities with the most at stake have the least capacity to deploy or govern the tools, and run their records, surveys, and institutional memory on borrowed platforms that leak.</p>
    <p class="punch">If digital infrastructure is becoming load-bearing for climate action, then who owns it is a <strong>climate-justice question</strong>. This lab treats youth not as respondents to that problem — <strong>but as its designers.</strong></p>
    <p class="foot-src">Sources: WRI/IIED Principles for Locally Led Adaptation · UNFCCC on AI in LDCs &amp; SIDS · UNDP Adaptation Innovation Marketplace</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 5 · What we're doing -->
<section class="slide">
  <div class="slide-inner">
    <span class="kicker">What we are doing</span>
    <h1>Listening, <em>structured.</em></h1>
    <p class="sub">Facilitated sessions on a platform we built for this room — not a survey, not a panel.</p>
    <div class="cards cols-2">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="tag is-lime">Consent first</span>
        <h3>Anonymous, withdrawable, optional</h3>
        <p>Research-review consent before anything is submitted; public findings are a separate opt-in. Review and <strong>withdraw any input from your own phone</strong>, any time. Phones optional — facilitators transcribe spoken answers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="tag is-lime">One question at a time</span>
        <h3>Join by QR, answer on your phone</h3>
        <p>The room moves together: each step is one structured prompt. Drafts save on your device; the facilitator's timer is mirrored to your screen.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="tag is-lime">Dissent is data</span>
        <h3>Vote, revise, disagree</h3>
        <p>Every submission goes on a two-column ballot — 3 votes per module per person, revisable after discussion. Consensus is recorded <strong>alongside dissent and unresolved questions.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="tag is-lime">Auditable</span>
        <h3>Every finding traces back</h3>
        <p>Session data exports to a structured report; the paper can trace every claim to a structured prompt put to a real room.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 6 · The two modules -->
<section class="slide">
